.SH NAME
ns_set \- Data Structure for Multimap with Potentially Case-insentitive Key-value Pairs
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBns_set array\fR \fIsetId\fR
.sp
\fBns_set cleanup\fR
.sp
\fBns_set copy\fR \fIsetId\fR
.sp
\fBns_set cput\fR ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? \fIsetId\fR \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR
.sp
\fBns_set create\fR ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? ?\fIname\fR? ?\fIkey\fR? ?\fIvalue\fR? \&.\&.\&.
.sp
\fBns_set delete\fR \fIsetId\fR \fIfieldNumber\fR
.sp
\fBns_set delkey\fR ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? \fIsetId\fR \fIkey\fR
.sp
\fBns_set find\fR ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? \fIsetId\fR \fIkey\fR
.sp
\fBns_set format\fR ?\fB-noname\fR? ?\fB-lead \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-separator \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?--? \fIsetId\fR
.sp
\fBns_set free\fR \fIsetId\fR
.sp
\fBns_set get\fR ?\fB-all\fR? ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? \fIsetId\fR \fIkey\fR ?\fIdefault\fR?
.sp
\fBns_set isnull\fR \fIsetId\fR \fIfieldNumber\fR
.sp
\fBns_set key\fR \fIsetId\fR \fIfieldNumber\fR
.sp
\fBns_set keys\fR \fIsetId\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
.sp
\fBns_set list\fR
.sp
\fBns_set merge\fR ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? \fIsetId1\fR \fIsetId2\fR
.sp
\fBns_set move\fR \fIsetId1\fR \fIsetId2\fR
.sp
\fBns_set name\fR \fIsetId\fR
.sp
\fBns_set put\fR \fIsetId\fR \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR
.sp
\fBns_set size\fR \fIsetId\fR ?\fInrElements\fR? ?\fIbufferSize\fR?
.sp
\fBns_set split\fR \fIsetId\fR ?\fIsplitChar\fR?
.sp
\fBns_set truncate\fR \fIsetId\fR \fIfieldNumber\fR
.sp
\fBns_set unique\fR ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? \fIsetId\fR \fIkey\fR
.sp
\fBns_set update\fR ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? \fIsetId\fR \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR
.sp
\fBns_set value\fR \fIsetId\fR \fIfieldNumber\fR
.sp
\fBns_set values\fR \fIsetId\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
.sp
.BE
.SH DESCRIPTION
This command provides functions for manipulating sets of key-value
pairs, or "fields"\&.  Fields in the set are ordered numerically,
starting at zero and increasing by one up to the total number of
fields\&.  An \fBns_set\fR is actually a multimap (also called
multiset, or bag), meaning the same key can appear
multiple times within the same set\&.
Optionally, sets can be defined to be case-insensitive, meaning that
mixed and uppercase keys are treated exactly like
lowercase keys (for details, see \fBCase Sensitivity\fR)\&.
.PP
The \fBns_set\fR data structure is especially useful for
handling data like HTTP headers, which are per definition case
insensitive and may have repeated keys\&.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBns_set array\fR \fIsetId\fR
Returns the contents of the \fIsetId\fR in a string format similar to
Tcl's array-get syntax\&.  This is helpful for iterating over
key-value pairs with \fBforeach\fR\&.  The result can also be used for
creating a Tcl \fBdict\fR or a Tcl \fBarray\fR\&. Note that since
\fBns_set\fR can contain duplicate keys, these conversion may
lose duplicates, as keys in dicts and arrays are unique\&.
.CS


 # Output HTTP request headers as string
 set headers [ns_conn headers]
 set output ""
  foreach {key value} [ns_set array $headers] {
    append output "$key = $value \\n"
  }
  set output

.CE
.TP
\fBns_set cleanup\fR
Deletes all sets in the current interpreter\&.
See also the section \fBStorage Management\fR below\&.
.TP
\fBns_set copy\fR \fIsetId\fR
Returns a new set with the same name and contents as the specified
\fIsetId\fR\&. See also the section \fBStorage Management\fR below\&.
.TP
\fBns_set cput\fR ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? \fIsetId\fR \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR
Conditionally appends a new field to the set with the specified
\fIkey\fR and \fIvalue\fR, only if the key does not already exist in the set\&.
Returns the field number of the newly added field, or the field
number of the existing field if it already exists\&.
.TP
\fBns_set create\fR ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? ?\fIname\fR? ?\fIkey\fR? ?\fIvalue\fR? \&.\&.\&.
Creates a new set and returns its \fIsetId\fR\&.
.sp
If a \fIname\fR is provided, it sets the name of the set\&. You
can also initialize the set with \fIkey\fR/\fIvalue\fR pairs by
providing them as additional arguments\&.
.sp
If the \fB-nocase\fR option is used, the set becomes
case-insensitive for all commands that accept a \fIkey\fR parameter\&.
Keys are stored in case-insensitive sets in lowercase and returned
in lowercase\&.
.CS


 # Create a case-insensitive set with initial values
 set mySet [ns_set create -nocase mySetName a 1 B 2 c 3]

.CE
.TP
\fBns_set delete\fR \fIsetId\fR \fIfieldNumber\fR
Deletes the field in the set at the specified field number\&.
.TP
\fBns_set delkey\fR ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? \fIsetId\fR \fIkey\fR
Removes the first occurrence of a field in the set with the
specified \fIkey\fR\&. Note that multiple fields with the same key may
exist; this command only removes the first occurrence\&.
.TP
\fBns_set find\fR ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? \fIsetId\fR \fIkey\fR
Returns the index of the first field in the specified \fIsetId\fR
with a matching \fIkey\fR\&. If no matching field is found, returns -1\&.
.TP
\fBns_set format\fR ?\fB-noname\fR? ?\fB-lead \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-separator \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?--? \fIsetId\fR
Formats the specified set in a human-friendly way and returns the
string as result\&. Option \fB-noname\fR will omit the name from
the output\&. When \fIlead\fR is specified, every key in the output
will be preceded by this lead string (default is "")\&. When
\fIseparator\fR is specified, every key in the output will be followed by
this separator string (default is ": ")\&.
.CS


 # Example of ns_set format
 % set mySet [ns_set create mySetName a 1 b 2 c 3]
 % ns_set format $mySet
 mySetName:
   a: 1
   b: 2
   c: 3

 # Using options
 % ns_set format -noname -lead "-> " -separator " = " $mySet
 -> a = 1
 -> b = 2
 -> c = 3

.CE
.TP
\fBns_set free\fR \fIsetId\fR
Frees (deletes) the specified set\&. Although sets are automatically
freed at the end of the request (see section
\fBStorage Management\fR), freeing sets manually can
save temporary memory within loops with many iterations\&.
.TP
\fBns_set get\fR ?\fB-all\fR? ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? \fIsetId\fR \fIkey\fR ?\fIdefault\fR?
Returns the first value associated with the specified \fIkey\fR\&. If the
key is not found, the default value or an empty string is returned\&.
With the \fB-all\fR option, all values for the key are returned
as a list\&.
.CS


 % set mySet [ns_set create mySetName a 1 b 2 c 3 a 4]
 # Get the first value for key 'a'
 % ns_set get $mySet a
 1

 # Get all values for key 'a' (if there are duplicates)
 % ns_set get -all $mySet a
 1 4

 # Case-insensitive get
 % ns_set get -nocase $mySet A
 1

.CE
.TP
\fBns_set isnull\fR \fIsetId\fR \fIfieldNumber\fR
Returns 1 if the field specified by \fIfieldNumber\fR is null, and 0
if it is not\&.  Note that an empty string is distinct from null; this
command returns 0 for an empty string\&.
.TP
\fBns_set key\fR \fIsetId\fR \fIfieldNumber\fR
Returns the key for the field at the specified \fIfieldNumber\fR\&.
.TP
\fBns_set keys\fR \fIsetId\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
Returns a list of all keys in the specified \fIsetId\fR\&. If a
\fIpattern\fR is provided, only keys matching the pattern are returned,
following Tcl's string match rules\&.
.TP
\fBns_set list\fR
Returns a list of all \fBns_set\fR instances in the current interpreter\&.
.TP
\fBns_set merge\fR ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? \fIsetId1\fR \fIsetId2\fR
Merges \fIsetId2\fR into \fIsetId1\fR\&. Fields from \fIsetId2\fR are
appended to \fIsetId1\fR only if their keys do not already exist in
\fIsetId1\fR\&.
.CS


 # Example of ns_set merge
 # Create two sets
 % set set1 [ns_set create key1 value1 key2 value2]
 % set set2 [ns_set create key2 value2_new key3 value3]

 # Merge set2 into set1
 % ns_set merge $set1 $set2

 # Now set1 contains key1, key2 (original value), and key3
 % ns_set keys $set1
 key1 key2 key3

.CE
.TP
\fBns_set move\fR \fIsetId1\fR \fIsetId2\fR
Moves all fields from \fIsetId2\fR to the end of \fIsetId1\fR,
leaving \fIsetId2\fR as an empty but valid set\&.
.CS


 # Example of ns_set move
 # Create two sets
 % set set1 [ns_set create key1 value1]
 % set set2 [ns_set create key2 value2 key3 value3]

 # Move fields from set2 to set1
 % ns_set move $set1 $set2

 # Now set1 contains key1, key2, key3
 % ns_set keys $set1
 key1 key2 key3

 # set2 is empty
 % ns_set size $set2
 0

.CE
.TP
\fBns_set name\fR \fIsetId\fR
Returns the name of the set or an empty string if no name has been
set\&.
.TP
\fBns_set put\fR \fIsetId\fR \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR
Appends a new field with the specified \fIkey\fR and \fIvalue\fR to
the set and returns the field number of the new field\&. Use
\fBns_set update\fR if you want to update a field with a certain key\&.
.TP
\fBns_set size\fR \fIsetId\fR ?\fInrElements\fR? ?\fIbufferSize\fR?
Returns the number of fields in the set\&.
.sp
The optional \fInrElements\fR can be used to change the
internal size of the set, the number of elements can be increased or
decreased\&. A decrease * might entail a truncation of the set\&.
.sp
The optional \fIbufferSize\fR can be used to change the
internal buffer size to either free some memory, when the
set was previously used to store many large objects, or to
pre-allocate storage to avoid resource operations in the internal
string buffer\&.
.TP
\fBns_set split\fR \fIsetId\fR ?\fIsplitChar\fR?
Splits one set into multiple sets based on a specific character,
\fIsplitChar\fR used in the key, and returns a list of
IDs for the newly created sets\&.
Defaults to using a period ("\&.") as the split character\&.
.CS


 # Create a set with keys containing periods
 % set s1 [ns_set create]
 % ns_set put $s1 dog\&.food "Yummy dog food!"
 % ns_set put $s1 cat\&.food "Yummy cat food!"

 % ns_set format $s1
 dog\&.food: Yummy dog food!
 cat\&.food: Yummy cat food!

 # Split the set into multiple (actually 2) sets based on '\&.' in the key
 % set newSets [ns_set split $s1]
 d2 d3

 # Show the result of the created new sets
 % set result ""
 % foreach s $newSets {
 %   append result [ns_set format $s]
 % }
 % set result
 dog:
   food: Yummy dog food!
 cat:
   food: Yummy cat food!

.CE
.TP
\fBns_set truncate\fR \fIsetId\fR \fIfieldNumber\fR
Removes all fields from \fIfieldNumber\fR onward\&.
.TP
\fBns_set unique\fR ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? \fIsetId\fR \fIkey\fR
Returns 1 if the specified \fIkey\fR is unique in the set and 0 if it is
not\&.
.CS


 # Create a set with duplicate keys
 % set headers [ns_set create]
 % ns_set put $headers Accept "text/html"
 % ns_set put $headers Accept "application/xml"
 % ns_set put $headers Host "www\&.example\&.com"

 # Check if 'Accept' header is unique
 % ns_set unique $headers Accept
 0

 # Check if 'Host' header is unique
 % ns_set unique $headers Host
 1

.CE
.TP
\fBns_set update\fR ?\fB-nocase\fR? ?--? \fIsetId\fR \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR
Updates the first occurrence of a field with the specified \fIkey\fR,
replacing its value with the new \fIvalue\fR\&. Equivalent to
\fBns_set delkey\fR followed by \fBns_set put\fR\&.
.TP
\fBns_set value\fR \fIsetId\fR \fIfieldNumber\fR
Returns the value of the field at the specified \fIfieldNumber\fR\&.
.TP
\fBns_set values\fR \fIsetId\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
Returns a list of all values in the set\&. If a \fIpattern\fR is specified,
only values matching the pattern are returned\&.
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
The option \fB-nocase\fR is valid for multiple commands\&.  The options
valid for just a single command are described above\&.
.TP
\fB-nocase\fR
When specified on a submethod accepting a \fIkey\fR the lookup for
the key will be case-insensitive, even if the underlying set is
case-sensitive\&.
.PP
.SH EXAMPLES
Basic operations on ns_sets:
.CS


 # Create an ns_set with several fields, named "mySetName"
 % set mySet [ns_set create mySetName a b c d e f A Joe B John C Jeff]
 % ns_set size $mySet
 6

 # Get the name of the ns_set
 % ns_set name $mySet
 mySetName

 # Get all keys and values of the ns_set
 % ns_set array $mySet
 a b c d e f A Joe B John C Jeff

 % ns_set keys $mySet
 a c e A B C

 % ns_set values $mySet
 b d f Joe John Jeff

 # Get the value of the field with key 'A'
 % ns_set get $mySet A
 Joe

 # Get the value of the field with key 'a'
 % ns_set get $mySet a
 b

 # Is the key 'a' unique in the ns_set?
 % ns_set unique $mySet a
 1

 # Is the key 'a' unique in the ns_set, when the case is ignored?
 % ns_set unique -nocase $mySet a
 0

 # Truncate the set after the third field
 % ns_set truncate $mySet 3
 % ns_set format $mySet
 mySetName:
   a: b
   c: d
   e: f

 # Update the value of the field with key "c"
 % ns_set update $mySet c "Hello World!"
 2
 % ns_set format $mySet
 mySetName:
   a: b
   c: Hello World!
   e: f

 # Lookup position of existing key
 % ns_set find $mySet c
 1

 # Lookup position of non-existing key
 % ns_set find $mySet nokey
 -1

 # Delete the field at position 0
 % ns_set delete $mySet 0
 % ns_set format $mySet
 mySetName:
   c: Hello World!
   e: f

.CE
.PP
Iterating over the fields of an ns_set:
.CS


 # Run through an ns_set based on key/value pairs
 foreach {key value} [ns_set array $myset]} {
   ns_log notice "key: $key, value: $value"
 }

 # Run through an ns_set based on indices
 for {set i 0} {$i < [ns_set size $myset]} {incr i} {
   set key [ns_set key $myset $i]
   set value [ns_set value $myset $i]
   ns_log notice "key: $key, value: $value"
 }

.CE
.SH "CASE SENSITIVITY"
.PP
Sets can be defined to be case-sensitive (default) or
case-insensitive\&. When a set is defined case-insensitive, all keys
are converted internally to lowercase\&. Every lookup operation with
an upper or mixed case key will then succeed when the key in
lowercase exists\&.
.PP
Case-sensitive and case-insensitive sets can be
created either from Tcl or from C\&.
To create a case-insensitive set from Tcl, use the option
\fB-nocase\fR for the \fBns_set create\fR command\&.
When a case-insensitive set is duplicated via \fBns_set copy\fR or
\fBns_set split\fR, the resulting set is also case-insensitive\&.
The sets used for HTTP request headers and configuration values are
created automatically from C as case-insensitive\&.
.PP
Even, when a set is defined as case-sensitive, all subcommands of
\fBns_set\fR receiving a key as input include the option \fB-nocase\fR,
to make a single lookup case-insensitive\&.
.PP
Legacy: previous versions of NaviServer have used special names for
the subcommands where the keys should be treated
case-insensitive\&. These subcommand names were prefixed with the
character "i" and were named "icput", "idelkey", "ifind", "iget",
"imerge", "iunique", and "iupdate"\&. These names are still supported
for compatibility, but it is recommended to use the option
\fB-nocase\fR instead\&.
.SH "STORAGE MANAGEMENT"
.PP
Sets created via the Tcl interface using \fBns_set\fR and the
subcommands \fBcreate\fR, \fBcopy\fR, and \fBsplit\fR are
automatically deleted after each request, or when
\fBns_set cleanup\fR is executed\&. The cleanup is performed
automatically after every request by the command \fBns_cleanup\fR\&.
.PP
It is also possible to delete a specified set with the command
\fBns_set free\fR\&.
.SH NOTES
.PP
NULL values, distinct from empty strings, are useful when
representing a database row as an \fBns_set\fR\&. Currently, obtaining
a NULL value in an \fBns_set\fR through the Tcl API can be
challenging\&.  NULL values may occur in sets created by
\fBns_parsequery\fR or by omitting the last value when seeding an
\fBns_set\fR in the create subcommand, among other scenarios\&.
NULL-valued fields can also be created through the C API\&.
.PP
\fBns_set\fR is intended for relatively small amounts of data\&.
Since keys are found by linear search, loading very large datasets
into an \fBns_set\fR may result in poor performance\&. In such cases,
consider using a Tcl array or dict structure instead\&.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ns_cleanup, ns_conn, ns_findset, ns_ictl, ns_parsequery
.SH KEYWORDS
bag, data structure, global built-in, multimap, multiset, ns_set
